-- Q1: medium / L3 — Nonqualified preferred stock as boot under 351(g)
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Delgado contributes equipment (basis $80,000, FMV $250,000) to a newly formed corporation for 60% of the common stock and nonqualified preferred stock with a FMV of $40,000. An unrelated investor contributes $170,000 cash for 40% of the common stock. Under Section 351(g), how should Delgado treat the nonqualified preferred stock?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The nonqualified preferred stock is treated as boot, and Delgado recognizes $40,000 gain", "The nonqualified preferred stock is treated as qualifying stock, and Delgado recognizes no gain", "The nonqualified preferred stock is excluded from the transaction entirely", "The nonqualified preferred stock triggers full gain recognition of $170,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 351(g) provides that nonqualified preferred stock is not treated as "stock" for Section 351 purposes. It is treated as boot, triggering gain recognition to the extent of its FMV ($40,000), which is less than the $170,000 realized gain. Wrong (B): Nonqualified preferred stock fails the definition of stock under 351(g) due to features like mandatory redemption or dividend preferences tied to interest rates. Wrong (C): The stock is not excluded; it remains part of the exchange but is reclassified as other property (boot). Wrong (D): Gain recognition is limited to the lesser of boot received ($40,000) or realized gain ($170,000), not the full realized gain.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L2 — Section 357(a) general rule: liability assumption is not boot
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Hadley transfers a building (basis $150,000, FMV $400,000) subject to a $100,000 mortgage to a new corporation for 100% of its stock in a qualifying Section 351 exchange. The liability does not exceed the property basis. Under Section 357(a), how is the assumed liability treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The assumed liability is treated as boot, triggering $100,000 of gain", "The assumed liability is not treated as boot and does not trigger gain recognition", "The assumed liability reduces the FMV of stock received but triggers partial gain", "The assumed liability is ignored entirely for all tax purposes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 357(a) provides that assumption of a liability by the corporation in a Section 351 exchange is generally not treated as boot. Since the $100,000 liability does not exceed Hadley's $150,000 basis, no gain is recognized under Section 357(c) either. Wrong (A): Under the general rule of 357(a), liabilities assumed are explicitly excluded from boot treatment unless an exception applies. Wrong (C): There is no partial gain concept when 357(a) applies; the liability assumption is simply disregarded as boot. Wrong (D): The liability is not ignored entirely — it reduces the transferor's basis in the stock received.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Section 704(c) built-in gain allocation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 704(c), when a partner contributes property with a built-in gain to a partnership, to whom must the pre-contribution gain be allocated when the property is later sold?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Equally among all partners based on their profit-sharing ratios", "To all partners in proportion to their capital account balances", "To the contributing partner", "To the partnership as an entity-level item not allocated to any partner"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 704(c) requires that built-in gain or loss existing at the time of contribution must be allocated to the contributing partner when the property is subsequently sold. This prevents shifting pre-contribution gain to other partners. Wrong (A): The profit-sharing ratio applies only to post-contribution appreciation, not the built-in gain at contribution. Wrong (B): Capital account balances do not determine allocation of pre-contribution gain under 704(c). Wrong (D): Partnerships are pass-through entities; all items are allocated to partners, and 704(c) specifically targets the contributing partner.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — Section 332 dual test: 80% voting AND value
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Westbrook Corp owns 90% of the voting stock of Ellison Inc. but only 75% of the total value of all Ellison stock outstanding. Ellison adopts a plan of complete liquidation. Under Section 332, does Westbrook qualify for tax-free subsidiary liquidation treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because Westbrook owns more than 80% of voting stock", "Yes, because Westbrook's combined ownership exceeds 80% on average", "No, because Section 332 is elective and Westbrook must affirmatively choose it", "No, because Westbrook must own at least 80% of both voting power and total value"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 332(b)(1) requires the parent to own at least 80% of the total voting power AND 80% of the total value of all outstanding stock of the subsidiary. Westbrook fails the value test (75%), so Section 332 does not apply. Wrong (A): Meeting the voting test alone is insufficient; both the voting power and value thresholds must be satisfied simultaneously. Wrong (B): There is no averaging concept; both tests must be independently met. Wrong (C): Section 332 is mandatory, not elective, when its requirements are satisfied — but here they are not met.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Section 737 gain on distribution to contributing partner
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Vance contributed land (basis $50,000, FMV $200,000) to a partnership two years ago for a 30% interest. The partnership now distributes $180,000 of marketable securities to Vance in a current distribution. Vance's outside basis before the distribution is $60,000. Under Section 737, what gain must Vance recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$120,000, the lesser of the excess distribution over basis or the net precontribution gain", "$150,000, the full built-in gain at the time of the original contribution", "$180,000, the full value of the distributed securities", "$0, because Section 731 protects partners from gain on property distributions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 737 requires the contributing partner to recognize gain equal to the lesser of: (1) the excess of the FMV of distributed property ($180,000) over the partner's outside basis ($60,000) = $120,000, or (2) the net precontribution gain ($200,000 - $50,000 = $150,000). The lesser amount is $120,000. Wrong (B): The full $150,000 built-in gain is the ceiling, not the required recognition; the excess distribution test yields a lower amount. Wrong (C): The full distribution value is not the measure; gain is limited to the excess over outside basis and capped at precontribution gain. Wrong (D): Section 731 is the general rule, but Section 737 overrides it when property is distributed to a partner who previously contributed built-in gain property within seven years.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Section 735 character of gain on distributed inventory
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A partnership distributes inventory (partnership basis $25,000, FMV $45,000) to partner Nakamura in a current distribution. Nakamura sells the inventory 18 months after the distribution for $48,000. Under Section 735, what is the character of Nakamura's gain on the sale?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Capital gain, because Nakamura held the inventory for more than one year after distribution", "Ordinary income, because inventory retains its ordinary character for five years after distribution", "Part ordinary income and part capital gain based on the holding period", "Capital gain, because the character is determined solely by Nakamura's holding period"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 735(a)(2) provides that inventory items distributed by a partnership retain their ordinary income character if sold within five years of the distribution date. Since Nakamura sold within five years, the entire gain is ordinary income regardless of Nakamura's holding period. Wrong (A): The partner's post-distribution holding period does not override the five-year taint period for inventory under Section 735. Wrong (C): There is no bifurcation; the entire gain is ordinary within the five-year window. Wrong (D): Section 735 specifically overrides the general holding period rules for distributed inventory and unrealized receivables.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Section 751 hot assets in partnership liquidating distribution
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Granger receives a liquidating distribution from a partnership consisting solely of $200,000 cash. The partnership holds substantial unrealized receivables (Section 751 "hot assets") that were allocated in part to Granger's interest. Granger's outside basis is $120,000. Under Section 751(b), how does the presence of hot assets affect the tax treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The entire $80,000 gain is treated as capital gain under Section 731", "The gain is bifurcated: the portion attributable to Granger's share of hot assets is ordinary income, and the remainder is capital gain", "The hot assets convert Granger's entire gain to ordinary income", "Section 751 does not apply to liquidating distributions, only to sales of partnership interests"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 751(b) applies when a partner receives a distribution that changes the partner's proportionate share of hot assets (unrealized receivables and inventory). When Granger receives only cash and gives up a share of hot assets, the deemed exchange triggers ordinary income treatment on the portion representing hot assets. Wrong (A): Section 731 alone would produce capital gain, but Section 751(b) overrides this for the hot asset component. Wrong (B): While bifurcation is conceptually correct, this answer understates the impact — when the partner surrenders all hot asset interest for cash, the ordinary income portion dominates. Wrong (D): Section 751(b) explicitly applies to distributions, not just sales of partnership interests under 751(a).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Section 351 control test: services plus nominal property
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Fenton and Garza form a corporation. Fenton contributes equipment (basis $120,000, FMV $300,000) for 60% of the stock. Garza performs consulting services valued at $175,000 for 35% of the stock and also contributes $500 cash for 5% of the stock. The IRS examines whether Garza's $500 cash contribution is de minimis relative to the services. If the IRS disregards Garza's property contribution, what is the impact on Section 351?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 351 still applies because Fenton alone owns 60%, exceeding the 50% threshold", "Section 351 fails entirely because the transferors of property hold only 65% control", "Section 351 fails because Fenton holds only 60% control, below the required 80%", "Section 351 applies because Garza's combined stock (40%) plus Fenton's (60%) exceeds 80%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): If the IRS disregards Garza's nominal $500 property contribution as de minimis, Garza is treated as contributing only services. A services-only transferor is excluded from the control group. Fenton, the sole property transferor, holds only 60% — below the 80% threshold required under Section 368(c). Section 351 fails, and Fenton must recognize the $180,000 gain. Wrong (A): The Section 351 control threshold is 80%, not 50%; Fenton's 60% alone is insufficient. Wrong (B): 65% is incorrect; Fenton holds 60% and Garza would be excluded entirely as a non-property transferor. Wrong (D): If Garza's property contribution is disregarded, Garza is not a transferor of property, so Garza's stock cannot count toward the 80% control requirement.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Partnership liability sharing increases outside basis
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Kimura contributes land (basis $60,000, FMV $200,000) subject to a $90,000 recourse mortgage to a general partnership for a 50% interest. The other partner contributes $110,000 cash. Under partnership formation rules, what is Kimura's outside basis in the partnership interest immediately after contribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$15,000, because Kimura's basis is reduced by the full liability and increased by the 50% share", "$60,000, because the carryover basis is not adjusted for liabilities", "$105,000, because Kimura adds the full liability to the carryover basis", "($30,000), because the liability exceeds basis creating a negative basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 722, Kimura's initial outside basis is the $60,000 carryover basis of the land. Under Section 752, Kimura is treated as receiving a distribution for the portion of liability shifted to the other partner (50% of $90,000 = $45,000 decrease) and contributing for the retained share (50% of $90,000 = $45,000). Net effect: $60,000 - $45,000 + $0 = $15,000. Wrong (B): Liabilities must be accounted for; the carryover basis is adjusted by the net liability shift under Section 752. Wrong (C): Kimura does not add the full liability — only the share Kimura retains, while the shifted portion reduces basis. Wrong (D): Partnership basis cannot go negative; if it did, gain would be recognized, but the calculation here yields a positive $15,000.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Section 306 stock taint on preferred in 351
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Whitfield contributes equipment (basis $75,000, FMV $300,000) to a corporation with $150,000 of accumulated E&P for 80% of the common stock and preferred stock with FMV of $60,000. The transfer qualifies under Section 351. If Whitfield later sells the preferred stock, how is the gain characterized under Section 306?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Capital gain, because Section 306 only applies to stock dividends", "Ordinary income to the extent of E&P existing at the time of the Section 351 exchange", "Tax-free return of capital up to Whitfield's basis, then capital gain", "Long-term capital gain if held more than one year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 306(c)(1)(B) defines Section 306 stock to include preferred stock received tax-free under Section 351 when the corporation had E&P. Upon disposition, Section 306(a)(1) treats the proceeds as ordinary income to the extent of the ratable share of E&P at the time of the 351 exchange. Wrong (A): Section 306 applies not only to stock dividends but also to preferred stock received in Section 351 and other tax-free exchanges. Wrong (C): The return of capital treatment applies only if Section 306 does not apply; here the preferred stock is tainted. Wrong (D): The holding period is irrelevant because Section 306(a) converts what would otherwise be capital gain into ordinary income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Section 346 partial liquidation definition
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 302(e), what test determines whether a corporate distribution qualifies as a partial liquidation eligible for sale or exchange treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A test applied at the shareholder level based on the shareholder's ownership percentage", "A test applied at the shareholder level based on the shareholder's holding period", "A test applied at the corporate level determining whether the distribution is not essentially equivalent to a dividend", "A test applied at the corporate level based on the corporation's debt-to-equity ratio"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 302(e) defines a partial liquidation as a distribution that is not essentially equivalent to a dividend, determined at the corporate level. The safe harbor requires cessation of a qualifying trade or business while continuing another. Wrong (A): Unlike stock redemption tests under 302(b)(1)-(3), the partial liquidation test focuses on corporate-level contraction, not shareholder-level ownership changes. Wrong (B): The shareholder's holding period is irrelevant to the partial liquidation determination. Wrong (D): The corporation's debt-to-equity ratio has no bearing on whether a distribution qualifies as a partial liquidation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Section 351 loss is never recognized
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 351, if a transferor contributes property with a fair market value less than its adjusted basis to a corporation in a qualifying exchange, what is the treatment of the built-in loss at the time of transfer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The transferor recognizes the loss immediately", "The transferor may elect to recognize the loss under Section 351(f)", "The loss is permanently disallowed and can never be claimed", "The loss is deferred — no loss is recognized, but it is preserved in the transferor's stock basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 351 provides nonrecognition for both gains and losses. No loss is recognized at the time of transfer. The loss is preserved through the transferor's substituted basis in the stock, which retains the higher adjusted basis of the contributed property and may be realized when the stock is later sold. Wrong (A): Section 351 prohibits recognition of both gain and loss on a qualifying transfer; the loss is deferred, not currently deductible. Wrong (B): There is no Section 351(f) election to recognize loss; nonrecognition is mandatory in a qualifying Section 351 exchange. Wrong (C): The loss is not permanently disallowed; it is preserved in the stock basis and may be realized upon a subsequent disposition of the stock.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Section 336(d)(2) anti-stuffing rule
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A CPA is advising Rutherford Corp, which adopted a plan of complete liquidation. Two years before adoption, shareholder Crane contributed equipment (basis $500,000, FMV $280,000) to Rutherford in a Section 351 exchange. The equipment's FMV at liquidation is $250,000. The CPA must determine the deductible loss. Under the anti-stuffing rule of Section 336(d)(2), what is the allowable loss on distribution of the equipment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$250,000 loss, using the original $500,000 basis and current $250,000 FMV", "$0 loss, because the built-in loss at contribution is entirely disallowed along with post-contribution decline", "$220,000 loss, limited to the built-in loss at the time of contribution only", "$30,000 loss, because the equipment basis is reduced to $280,000 FMV at contribution"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 336(d)(2) is an anti-stuffing provision that reduces the basis of contributed built-in loss property to its FMV at the time of contribution ($280,000). The allowable loss is limited to the post-contribution decline: $280,000 - $250,000 = $30,000. The $220,000 built-in loss at contribution is stripped out. Wrong (A): The full $250,000 loss using the original $500,000 basis is exactly what Section 336(d)(2) prevents by reducing basis to contribution-date FMV. Wrong (B): The loss is not entirely disallowed; the post-contribution decline of $30,000 remains deductible because it represents genuine economic loss to the corporation. Wrong (C): The $220,000 built-in loss at contribution is the disallowed portion, not the allowable portion; the allowable loss is the decline occurring after contribution.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Depreciation recapture preserved but deferred in 351
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Monroe contributes equipment to a new corporation under Section 351. The equipment has an adjusted basis of $40,000, original cost of $120,000, and FMV of $95,000. Monroe claimed $80,000 of depreciation. Under Section 1245, what happens to the potential depreciation recapture at the time of the Section 351 transfer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Monroe recognizes $55,000 of ordinary recapture income immediately", "The recapture potential is eliminated permanently by the nonrecognition provision", "The recapture potential transfers to the corporation and is triggered if the corporation later sells the equipment at a gain", "Monroe recognizes $80,000 of recapture income because Section 1245 overrides Section 351"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 1245(b)(3), no depreciation recapture is triggered on a Section 351 transfer. The recapture potential is not eliminated — it carries over to the corporation through the carryover basis. If the corporation later sells the equipment at a gain, the $80,000 of recapture potential may be triggered as ordinary income at that time. Wrong (A): Section 1245(b)(3) specifically exempts Section 351 transfers from current recapture recognition; no gain is recognized at the time of transfer. Wrong (B): The recapture is deferred, not permanently eliminated — it transfers to the corporation through the carryover basis rules and is triggered upon a subsequent taxable disposition. Wrong (D): Section 1245 does not override Section 351; instead, 1245(b)(3) provides a specific exception for nonrecognition transfers while preserving the recapture potential.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L2 — Section 332 minority shareholders get 331 treatment
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Arden Corp owns 85% and individual investor Liu owns 15% of Belmont Inc. Belmont adopts a plan of complete liquidation and distributes all assets. Liu's stock basis is $50,000 and the FMV of assets Liu receives is $90,000. Under which section is Liu's distribution taxed?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 332, with no gain recognized", "Section 336, with gain recognized at the corporate level", "Section 331, with $40,000 capital gain recognized", "Section 334(b)(1), with a carryover basis in the assets"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 332 applies only to the parent corporation owning 80% or more. Minority shareholders like Liu are treated under Section 331, which treats the liquidating distribution as a sale or exchange of stock. Liu recognizes $40,000 capital gain ($90,000 FMV - $50,000 basis). Wrong (A): Section 332's nonrecognition treatment is limited to the 80%+ parent; minority shareholders do not qualify. Wrong (B): Section 336 governs the corporation's gain or loss on distributed property, not the shareholder's treatment. Wrong (D): Section 334(b)(1) determines the parent's basis in assets received under Section 332, not the minority shareholder's treatment.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q16: hard / L3 — Section 362(e)(2) election to reduce stock basis instead
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Palmer contributes two properties to a new corporation for 100% of the stock under Section 351. Property X has basis $300,000 and FMV $400,000 (built-in gain of $100,000). Property Y has basis $250,000 and FMV $100,000 (built-in loss of $150,000). The aggregate net built-in loss is $50,000. Under Section 362(e)(2), the corporation's basis in Property Y would normally be reduced to $100,000. However, Palmer and the corporation jointly elect under Section 362(e)(2)(C). What is the effect of this election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The corporation takes full carryover basis in both properties, but Palmer must recognize $50,000 of gain", "Property Y's basis is reduced to FMV regardless of the election", "The corporation takes full carryover basis in both properties, and Palmer reduces stock basis by $50,000 instead", "The election eliminates the net built-in loss by increasing Property Y's FMV to $150,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 362(e)(2)(C) allows the transferor and corporation to jointly elect to reduce the transferor's stock basis (rather than the corporation's asset basis) by the net built-in loss amount. The corporation keeps carryover basis ($300,000 for X, $250,000 for Y), and Palmer's stock basis is reduced by $50,000. Wrong (A): The election does not trigger gain recognition; it shifts the basis reduction from the corporate asset to the shareholder's stock. Wrong (B): The election under 362(e)(2)(C) specifically overrides the default rule that would reduce Property Y's basis to FMV. Wrong (D): The election does not change FMV or eliminate the economic loss; it merely shifts where the basis reduction is applied — to the stock rather than the asset.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Section 721 vs 351: no control requirement for partnerships
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$An investor joins an established 10-member partnership by contributing appreciated real estate in exchange for a 5% interest. The existing partners retain 95% collectively. A colleague suggests the transfer might be taxable because the investor receives less than 80% ownership. Is the colleague correct?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, the 80% control rule applies to all entity formations and the transfer is fully taxable", "Yes, partnership formations require at least 50% ownership for nonrecognition treatment", "No, Section 721 provides nonrecognition for property-for-interest exchanges regardless of the percentage received", "No, but only because the investor is joining an existing partnership rather than forming a new one"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 721 provides nonrecognition when property is contributed to a partnership in exchange for a partnership interest. Unlike Section 351 for corporations, there is no minimum ownership or control requirement. The investor recognizes no gain regardless of the 5% interest received. Wrong (A): The 80% control rule is exclusive to Section 351 corporate formations and has no application to partnerships under Section 721. Wrong (B): No minimum ownership percentage exists for partnership formations under Section 721; nonrecognition applies at any ownership level. Wrong (D): Section 721 applies equally to new and existing partnerships; the distinction between new and existing is irrelevant to nonrecognition treatment.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Section 331 holding period determines character
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Sinclair purchased stock in Waverly Corp for $40,000 eight months ago. Waverly adopts a plan of complete liquidation and distributes $75,000 in assets to Sinclair. Under Section 331, what is the character of Sinclair's $35,000 gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Ordinary income, because liquidating distributions are always ordinary", "Short-term capital gain, because Sinclair held the stock for one year or less", "Long-term capital gain, because liquidating distributions receive preferential treatment", "Dividend income to the extent of Waverly's accumulated E&P"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 331, a liquidating distribution is treated as a sale or exchange of stock. The gain's character depends on the shareholder's holding period. Since Sinclair held the stock for only eight months (not more than one year), the $35,000 gain is short-term capital gain. Wrong (A): Section 331 provides sale or exchange treatment, producing capital gain or loss, not ordinary income. Wrong (C): While liquidating distributions receive capital treatment, the long-term or short-term classification depends on the actual holding period — here, only eight months. Wrong (D): Section 331 explicitly overrides dividend treatment; the distribution is treated as received in exchange for stock, not as a corporate distribution subject to E&P analysis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Partnership encumbered property: net liability shift
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Ortega contributes property (basis $100,000, FMV $350,000) subject to a $130,000 nonrecourse liability to a partnership for a 40% interest. The partnership has no other liabilities. Under Section 752, what is the net effect of the liability on Ortega's outside basis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Increase of $130,000, because Ortega retains all liability responsibility", "Decrease of $130,000, because the full liability shifts to the partnership", "Decrease of $78,000, representing the 60% share shifted to other partners", "No effect, because nonrecourse liabilities are not considered in outside basis calculations"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 752, when Ortega contributes encumbered property, Ortega is treated as receiving a distribution for the portion shifted to other partners. Ortega's share of the $130,000 liability is 40% ($52,000), so $78,000 (60%) shifts to other partners, reducing Ortega's basis by $78,000. Net outside basis: $100,000 - $78,000 = $22,000. Wrong (A): Ortega does not retain full responsibility; the liability is shared among all partners based on their interests. Wrong (B): The full liability does not reduce Ortega's basis; Ortega retains a 40% share, so only the 60% shifted to others reduces basis. Wrong (D): Nonrecourse liabilities are allocated among partners under Section 752 and do affect outside basis calculations.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Section 351 stock warrants as boot
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Brandt contributes property (basis $50,000, FMV $180,000) to a new corporation in a Section 351 exchange. In addition to common stock, Brandt receives warrants to purchase additional shares, with a FMV of $20,000. Under Section 351, how are the stock warrants treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As qualifying stock that satisfies the control requirement", "As additional equity not subject to tax", "As property received by Brandt with no tax consequence", "As boot (other property), requiring gain recognition of $20,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Stock warrants and stock rights are not treated as "stock" for purposes of Section 351. They constitute "other property" (boot), and Brandt must recognize gain equal to the lesser of boot received ($20,000) or realized gain ($130,000). Recognized gain is $20,000. Wrong (A): Warrants are explicitly excluded from the definition of stock under Section 351; they do not count toward the control requirement. Wrong (B): Warrants are taxable boot, not tax-free equity, in a Section 351 exchange. Wrong (C): The warrants are "other property" received by the transferor, which triggers gain recognition under the boot rules.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Section 337 no gain/loss on distribution to 80% parent
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 337, when a subsidiary liquidates into an 80%-or-more parent corporation under Section 332, what gain or loss does the subsidiary recognize on the distribution of its assets?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain or loss is recognized by the subsidiary on distributions to the parent", "Gain is recognized but loss is not", "Both gain and loss are recognized as if assets were sold at FMV", "Loss is recognized but gain is not"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 337(a) provides that no gain or loss is recognized by the liquidating subsidiary on distributions of property to an 80%-or-more parent corporation in a Section 332 liquidation. This complements the parent's nonrecognition under Section 332. Wrong (B): Section 337 provides nonrecognition for both gains and losses on distributions to the qualifying parent, not just losses. Wrong (C): This describes the general rule under Section 336(a) for non-subsidiary liquidations, not the Section 337 exception for distributions to an 80%+ parent. Wrong (D): Section 337 provides complete nonrecognition — neither gain nor loss — on distributions to the parent in a qualifying subsidiary liquidation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Assignment of income doctrine in Section 351 context
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A CPA is advising Jennings, a cash-basis attorney who plans to incorporate her solo practice under Section 351. Her assets include $180,000 in accounts receivable (zero tax basis) and office equipment (basis $40,000, FMV $60,000). She will receive 100% of the stock. A colleague warns that the assignment of income doctrine could override Section 351 for the receivables. The CPA must evaluate whether Jennings should contribute the receivables or collect them before incorporation. Which analysis is most accurate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The assignment of income doctrine fully overrides Section 351, and Jennings must recognize $180,000 of ordinary income upon transfer", "Contributing the receivables under Section 351 is generally permissible because accounts receivable are property; the income is recognized by the corporation as collected, preserving overall tax neutrality", "Jennings should collect the receivables before incorporating to avoid the assignment of income doctrine, which always applies to zero-basis receivables", "The receivables cannot be contributed under Section 351 because they are not considered property"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Accounts receivable are treated as "property" for Section 351 purposes. The IRS generally respects the contribution under Section 351, and the income is recognized by the corporation when the receivables are collected. The corporation takes a zero carryover basis, so the full amount is income when collected. Wrong (A): The assignment of income doctrine does not fully override Section 351 for receivables; courts have generally upheld the transfer when genuine incorporation occurs. Wrong (C): While collecting first is a conservative approach, it is not required — Section 351 accommodates zero-basis receivables as property. Wrong (D): Accounts receivable, including zero-basis receivables of a cash-basis taxpayer, are property for Section 351 purposes.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Section 338 regular (buyer-only) phantom double tax
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A CPA is advising Regent Corp, which acquired 100% of Dover Inc. (a C corporation) for $3,000,000 in a qualified stock purchase. Dover's assets have an aggregate basis of $1,200,000 and FMV of $3,000,000. The selling shareholders have a stock basis of $900,000. Regent is considering a unilateral Section 338 election (not a joint 338(h)(10) election). The CPA must evaluate the economic consequences. What is the critical drawback of a regular Section 338 election compared to a Section 338(h)(10) election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A regular 338 election does not provide a step-up in asset basis to the buyer", "A regular 338 election triggers a deemed asset sale at the target corporation level AND the selling shareholders still report gain on the stock sale, creating a double tax", "A regular 338 election requires the selling shareholders to consent, making it impractical", "A regular 338 election only applies to S corporation targets, not C corporations"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): In a regular (buyer-only) Section 338 election, the target corporation is deemed to have sold its assets at FMV, triggering corporate-level tax on $1,800,000 of gain. The selling shareholders still report their stock sale gain ($3,000,000 - $900,000 = $2,100,000). This "phantom" double tax makes regular 338 elections economically unattractive for C corporation targets. Wrong (A): A regular 338 election does provide a step-up in asset basis to the buyer — that is its purpose — but the double tax cost typically outweighs the benefit. Wrong (C): A regular Section 338 election is made unilaterally by the buyer; it does not require seller consent. The 338(h)(10) election is the one requiring joint consent. Wrong (D): Regular Section 338 elections apply to any corporate target, not just S corporations; 338(h)(10) is more commonly used with S corporation targets.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Section 332 requires plan of liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Holbrook Corp owns 100% of Merritt Inc. Merritt distributes all of its assets to Holbrook in a single transaction without formally adopting a plan of liquidation or filing Form 966. Holbrook claims Section 332 tax-free treatment. Is Holbrook's position correct?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because the 80% ownership alone is sufficient for Section 332", "Yes, because formal adoption of a plan is optional when there is only one distribution", "No, because only distributions pursuant to a plan of liquidation with an IRS ruling qualify", "No, because Section 332(b) requires that the distribution be pursuant to a plan of liquidation adopted by the subsidiary"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 332(b) requires that the corporation be completely liquidated pursuant to a plan of complete liquidation. While formal documentation requirements have been relaxed, the subsidiary must adopt a plan and the distributions must be made pursuant to that plan. Without a plan, Section 332 may not apply. Wrong (A): The 80% ownership test is necessary but not sufficient; a plan of liquidation must also be adopted. Wrong (B): Even a single-distribution liquidation requires adoption of a plan; the number of distributions is irrelevant to the plan requirement. Wrong (C): An IRS private letter ruling is not required; the plan must simply be adopted by the subsidiary's board.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Section 351 "immediately after" and prearranged dispositions
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A CPA advises Mercer, who contributes property (basis $100,000, FMV $500,000) to a new corporation for 100% of the stock under Section 351. Before the contribution, Mercer has a binding agreement to sell 70% of the stock to an unrelated buyer immediately after formation. The CPA must evaluate whether the prearranged sale invalidates the Section 351 exchange. If the IRS successfully applies the step transaction doctrine and treats the contribution and sale as integrated, what is the most likely recharacterization?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The entire transaction is recharacterized as a taxable sale of property by Mercer, recognizing $400,000 gain", "Section 351 still applies because Mercer momentarily held 100% control", "The transaction is split: 30% qualifies under Section 351 and 70% is a taxable sale", "Section 351 applies to the full transfer, but Mercer recognizes capital gain on the subsequent stock sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the step transaction doctrine, the IRS can collapse the contribution and prearranged sale into a single integrated transaction. If Mercer never truly had control (because the sale was prearranged), the "immediately after" control requirement of Section 351 fails, and the entire property transfer is recharacterized as a taxable sale with $400,000 gain. Wrong (B): Momentary control is disregarded when there is a binding prearranged agreement to dispose of the controlling interest. Wrong (C): The step transaction doctrine does not bifurcate the transaction; it either collapses the steps into one or respects them as separate. Wrong (D): If the step transaction doctrine applies, Section 351 does not apply at all — the contribution itself is recharacterized as taxable.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Section 336(a) general rule: corporation recognizes gain/loss
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 336(a), what is the general rule for a corporation that distributes property in a complete liquidation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The corporation recognizes no gain or loss on the distribution", "The corporation recognizes gain or loss as if the property were sold at fair market value", "The corporation recognizes gain but not loss on the distribution", "The corporation's gain or loss is deferred until the shareholder sells the property"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 336(a) provides that a liquidating corporation recognizes gain or loss on the distribution of property in complete liquidation as if the property were sold to the distributee at its fair market value. This is the general rule for corporate-level liquidation treatment. Wrong (A): This describes Section 337 treatment in a Section 332 subsidiary liquidation to the parent, not the general rule under Section 336(a). Wrong (C): Section 336(a) allows recognition of both gains and losses, subject to certain limitations under 336(d) for related-party losses. Wrong (D): There is no deferral mechanism; the corporation's gain or loss is recognized at the time of the liquidating distribution.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Transferor's holding period in stock tacks under 351
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Calloway contributes a capital asset held for six years (basis $90,000, FMV $220,000) to a new corporation for 100% of the stock under Section 351. Three months after formation, Calloway sells all the stock for $230,000. Under Section 1223(1), what is Calloway's holding period for the stock, and what is the character of the $140,000 gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Three months; $140,000 short-term capital gain", "Six years; $140,000 ordinary income", "Six years and three months; $140,000 long-term capital gain", "Three months for the appreciation and six years for the carryover portion"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 1223(1), when stock is received in a Section 351 exchange, the holding period of the contributed property tacks onto the stock. Calloway's holding period includes the six years holding the capital asset plus three months holding the stock, totaling over one year. The $140,000 gain ($230,000 - $90,000 basis) is long-term capital gain. Wrong (A): The holding period is not limited to the time Calloway held the stock; Section 1223(1) allows tacking of the contributed property's holding period. Wrong (B): The gain on sale of stock is capital in character, not ordinary, because stock is a capital asset. Wrong (D): There is no bifurcation of holding period; the entire stock basis takes a single tacked holding period under Section 1223(1).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
